<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Fronde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1648">1648</a>–<a href="/years/1653">1653</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Fronde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fronde was a series of revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between 1648 and 1653 during the minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. It began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> resisting new taxes imposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> and grew into a rebellion of great nobles led by princes such as Condé. The royal family twice fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, but the revolts failed, and memories of the disorder encouraged Louis XIV's later absolutism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-fronde.jpg" alt="The Fronde" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combat_de_deux_cavaliers_lors_de_la_Fronde,_faubourg_Saint-Antoine,_sous_les_murs_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Fronde arose from the financial strain of French involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, which ended in 1648, and of the continuing Franco-Spanish War, which began in 1635. Paying for the wars forced the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> to raise taxes, most of which fell on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, whose power base lay in the regional Parlements.</p><p>The most important, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>, refused to register the new decrees as an assault on established rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> had become king in 1643 at the age of five, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was ruled by a regency dominated by his mother, Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and Mazarin. Nobles who had lost influence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> at first supported the Parlements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first phase, the Fronde of the Parlements, began in May 1648, when a tax on judicial officers led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> to demand constitutional reforms. After Prince Louis II de Condé's victory at Lens in August, Mazarin arrested the leaders of the Parlement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> rose and barricaded its streets. The court fled the city on the night of 22 October. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> then freed the army, Condé besieged Paris, and the Peace of Rueil of 11 March 1649 ended this phase after little bloodshed.</p><p>The second phase, the Fronde of the Princes, lasted from 1650 to 1653 and was a scramble for power among discontented nobles, among them Gaston, Duke of Orleans, Condé, his brother the Prince of Conti and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-la-tour-d-auvergne-viscount-of-turenne">Viscount of Turenne</a>. Mazarin arrested Condé, Conti and Longueville on 14 January 1650, and Turenne led a rebellion against the court, which ended with his defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a> in December 1650. When Mazarin returned in December 1651 the war began again, with Turenne and Condé on opposite sides. The royal army trapped Condé's forces in the Faubourg St. Antoine on 2 July 1652, and the king entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 21 October 1652.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mazarin returned unopposed in February 1653. Tired of the turmoil and disgusted with the princes, the country came to look to the king and his party as the representatives of order, and the Fronde is thought to have prepared the absolutism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. Condé entered the service of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the war with Spain continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Peace of the Pyrenees</a> on 5 November 1659. On 27 January 1660 Condé obtained the forgiveness of Louis XIV, and the later careers of Turenne and Condé were as obedient subjects of their king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Fronde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cardinal Richelieu, chief minister of Louis XIII from 1624 to 1642, and Cardinal Mazarin after him extended the use of intendants, royal commissioners in the provinces, and Mazarin survived the revolt of the <b>Fronde</b> (1648–1653).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>The Fronde</b>, a series of revolts against royal authority from 1648 to 1653, left Louis XIV determined to rule alone.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
